<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deck the Halls</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">English words <a href="/years/1862">1862</a>; melody published <a href="/years/1794">1794</a>, first found in a manuscript of the 1700s</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Deck the Hall</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Deck the Halls&quot; is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-carol">Christmas carol</a> with a Welsh tune and English words. The melody belongs to &quot;Nos Galan&quot; (&quot;New Year's Eve&quot;), a traditional Welsh winter carol.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The English words were written by the Scottish musician Thomas Oliphant and first appeared in 1862, in volume 2 of Welsh Melodies, a set of four volumes by John Thomas that also gave Welsh words by John Jones (Talhaiarn). Some early sources call Oliphant's text a translation of Talhaiarn's Welsh, but it is not one in any strict sense. Talhaiarn's first verse begins &quot;Goreu pleser ar nos galan&quot;, &quot;The best pleasure on new year's eve&quot;, and speaks of house, fire, family, blonde ale and the voice of the harp. The title was singular, &quot;Deck the Hall&quot;, until about 1892.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Oliphant's original verses speak of boughs of holly, filling the meadcup and draining the barrel, striking the harp and hailing the new year, each line answered by a &quot;Fa, la, la&quot; refrain. A variant printed in the Pennsylvania School Journal in December 1877 and again four years later in The Franklin Square Song Collection drops the references to drinking and reads &quot;Don we now our gay apparel&quot; and &quot;See the blazing yule before us&quot;. Other common changes replace &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a>&quot; with &quot;Yuletide&quot;. The phrase &quot;'Tis the season&quot;, from the first verse, has become a byword for the Christmas season.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The melody belongs to &quot;Nos Galan&quot; (&quot;New Year's Eve&quot;), a traditional Welsh carol published in 1794, though much older than that. The tune was first found in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> by the Welsh harpist John Parry, dating from the 1700s, and he published it in British Harmony. The earliest published words for &quot;Nos Galan&quot; are a love song beginning &quot;O mor gynnes mynwes meinwen&quot; (&quot;Oh! how soft my fair one's bosom&quot;). The poet John Ceiriog Hughes later wrote his own lyrics, and folk singers added a middle verse.</p><p>Through the 18th century the tune spread widely. Mozart is said to have used it in his 18th violin sonata (1778), and Haydn arranged it under the Welsh title in 1803. In 1912 Ruth Herbert Lewis made a wax cylinder recording of a Welshman, Benjamin Davies, singing &quot;Can y Coach faier&quot; to the old melody, which can be heard in the British Library Sound Archive. The music has an AABA form, and the third and fourth &quot;Fa la la&quot; lines sung in English differ from those played in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Deck the Halls” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
